Pure Tcl 9.0 JSON library

package require json-lib

Creates ::json::json ensemble

A JSON-value is a Tcl list with a type and value (or just a type for null, true,
false).

{0}		null
{t}		true
{f}		false
{s string}	string is a normal Tcl un-escaped string
{n number}	number is a Tcl numeric value
{a list}	list is a Tcl list of JSON-values
{o dict}	dict is a Tcl dict with un-escaped strings for keys, JSON-value
		for the associated value
{j json}	json is a JSON string, pre-encoded for efficiency

Example:
::json::json pretty [::json::json encode \
	{o {array {a {{n 3.14} {s {Tcl string}} t} null 0}]

returns:
{
  "array": [
    3.14,
    "Tcl string",
    true
  ]
}

::json::json::decode {{"array":[3.14,"Tcl string",true]}}

returns:
o {array {a {{n 3.14} {s {Tcl string}} t}}}

Parsing, JSON string to JSON-value:
parse json ?index?
	Parse the JSON string in json, return a JSON-value and an index
	If index isn't specified, it defaults to 0.
	The returned index will skip over any trailing whitespace after a valid
	JSON item.  If no valid JSON is found, it will return an empty list and
	the index of the first non-whitespace character.
decode	json ?varname?
	If varname is not specified, then decode will return the
	JSON-value of the JSON string.	An error will be thrown if there
	are any trailing non-whitespace characters following, or if it
	is not a valid JSON string.  If a variable name is provided,
	the JSON-value will be returned in that variable and the rest
	of the string will be returned as the result (skipping any trailing
	whitespace).
decode-multi json
	Takes a string with multiple JSON strings, optionally separated by
	whitespace. Returns a list with all of the corresponding JSON-values;
	throws an error if any characters remain (i.e. not valid JSON).

Encoding, JSON-value to JSON string:
encode	jval
	Returns the JSON string corresponding to the JSON-value, without any
	extra whitespace.  Does not validate the structure.
encode-multi jval_list
	Returns a JSON string for each JSON-value in the provided list,
	separated by newlines.

Validation of JSON-value and number string:
valid	jval
	Validate a JSON-value, recursively validating nested values in objects
	and arrays.  Returns either "valid" or an error string.
valid-number value
	Validates that the value is a valid JSON number.  Tcl numbers should
	always be valid JSON numbers.  There is no attempt to validate the
	conversion of JSON and Tcl numbers; a number parsed from a JSON string
	will not be modified or converted.  Returns "valid" or an error string.

Formatting, add whitespace to a JSON string (not a JSON-value)
pretty	string ?tab? ?indent?
	Add whitespace to pretty-print a JSON string.  tab is how much to
	indent each level, indent is the initial indentation string.  The
	default for tab is two spaces, the default for indent is empty.
	No other conversions are done (strings and numbers will remain in
	the same format they were).  If the string is not valid JSON, it will
	throw an error.  The return value will be the formatted string.  This
	will process multiple JSON items in the string.

Utility routines:
get	jval ?selector ...?
	Get the JSON-value after recursively walking a structure.  The selector
	is a key for an object, or an index for an array.
getdef	default jval ?selector ...?
	Gets a value the same as get, but returns the default jval if any
	errors (invalid structure, missing key, invalid index)
type	jval ?selector ...?
	Get the type of the item after walking the structure as with get.
	Returns "null" "boolean" "number" "string" "array" "object" "json" or
	"invalid"
value	jval ?selector ...?
	Get the value of the item after walking the structure.
array-size jval
	Return the size of the array, error if not a valid array
object-names
	Return a list of object names (Tcl strings), error if not a valid object
extract	jval type ?selector ...?
	Walk the object, as with get, then extract a uniform array or
	object into a corresponding Tcl list or dict made up of Tcl
	values, not JSON-values.

Constructors:
array	?jval ...?
	Create an array from the arguments passed (which must be valid
	JSON-values)
array-add varname ?jval ...?
	Append items to an existing array, error if it is not a valid array.
array-list list
	Make a Tcl list of JSON-values into an array
object	?name jval ...?
	Create an object with pairs of names (Tcl strings) and JSON-values.
object-add varname ?name jval ...?
	Add items to an existing object, error if it is not a valid object.
object-dict dict
	Make a Tcl dict (with Tcl keys and JSON-values) into an object
null	Returns the constant "null" JSON-value
true	Returns the constant "true" JSON-value
false	Returns the constant "false" JSON-value
boolean	bool
	Returns a JSON-value for true or false based on a Tcl boolean
number	value
	Returns a JSON-value number with the value given - throws an error if
	the value specified is not a valid JSON format number.
string	value
	Returns a JSON-value string
json-embed jval
	Returns an embedded JSON string (pre-formatted encoded JSON)
json-array list
	Returns an embedded JSON array from a list of JSON strings.
